========================================================================
cbbench -- the timing instrument for the register-allocation
and scheduling sections.  It executes what those sections
DECIDE; it does not decide anything itself.
========================================================================

========================================================================
SECTION 1 -- THE INSTRUMENT.  Nothing below this point is a
claim yet.
========================================================================

THE CLOCK.  Measured twice: once busy, once across a 400 ms
sleep.  A TSC is a FIXED-RATE counter, so if the two agree then
a tick is TIME and never a cycle count, and every cost in this
file is a RATIO rather than a number of cycles.

  TSC rate, busy               2.2957 GHz
  TSC rate, across sleep       2.2957 GHz
  drift                       +0.0005%   (busy is the reference)
  VERDICT: invariant -- a tick is TIME.

THE FLOOR OF THE ESTIMATOR, measured on TWO bodies on purpose.
They are the same loop shape and they differ in what they wait
on.

  estimator = min-of-16, 200000 iterations, two bodies:
     a POINTER CHASE, 8192 nodes, L2-resident      2.157 ticks/op
     an ARITHMETIC increment loop                 1.586 ticks/op
     the two differ by                         -26.5%

  WHY TWO.  An arithmetic loop is bounded by the CORE CLOCK and
  the TSC is FIXED-RATE, so a clock ramp reads as noise that is
  really the clock.  A pointer chase spends its time WAITING on
  a load, and a clock change does not shorten it.  The chase is
  the floor this file quotes; the arithmetic loop is printed so
  the gap is on the page.
  WHAT A BENCHMARK'S NOISE USUALLY IS TELLS YOU WHICH PART OF
  THE MACHINE THE BENCHMARK IS MEASURING.

  pinned to cpu2, and the cpu that answered is 2  (VERIFIED)

  RDPMC in a forked child: KILLED by signal 11
  /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid = 4

  CONSEQUENCE, and it governs every number in sections 2 and 3:
    there is NO EVENT COUNTER.  Nothing here is a count of
    instructions, of cycles, of a branch taken or of a branch
    MISPREDICTED.  Every number is a DURATION, and a duration
    bounds a count without measuring it.

  iterations per arm: 200000   copies per arm: 16, the MINIMUM is kept

========================================================================
SECTION 2 -- THE COST OF A REGISTER ALLOCATION, IN REAL
CYCLES OF REAL MEMORY TRAFFIC.  Four arms, one function,
four values forced through a stack slot.
========================================================================

Every arm computes the SAME thing over the SAME data and
they must agree on the checksum.  They differ in one thing
only: how many intermediate values live in a register.

  arm   spilled   ticks/op   pass2-pass1   vs arm A   checksum
  A pressure 0        0      2.790    0.929         1.00x   5925179420309322629
  B pressure 1        1      3.346    0.837         1.20x   5925179420309322629
  C pressure 2        2      3.320    0.846         1.19x   5925179420309322629
  D pressure 4        4      3.905    0.976         1.40x   5925179420309322629

  TWO FLOORS, BOTH PRINTED, AND THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT
  QUESTIONS. §KEEP§THE CHASE IS THE FLOOR OF A *SINGLE
  MEASUREMENT* -- HOW CLOSE TWO RUNS OF THE SAME ARM CAN
  GET. §KEEP§ THE PASS-TO-PASS COLUMN IS THE FLOOR OF THE
  *ESTIMATOR THAT IS ACTUALLY USED* -- EACH ARM IS MEASURED
  TWICE, AS TWO INDEPENDENT MIN-OF-16 ESTIMATES, AND THE
  COLUMN IS HOW FAR THOSE TWO MINIMA LAND FROM EACH OTHER.
  §KEEP§ IT IS NOT MAX-MINUS-MIN OVER THE COPIES OF ONE
  MEASUREMENT: §KEEP§ THE MINIMUM HAS ALREADY DISCARDED THE
  SLOW COPIES, AND CHARGING THE MINIMUM FOR OUTLIERS IT
  REJECTED MADE THE FLOOR 1.08 TICK/OP, LARGER THAN THE
  0.71 TICK/OP DIFFERENCE IT WAS SUPPOSED TO GOVERN, AND
  §KEEP§ THE REPORT THEREFORE CONCLUDED THAT SPILLING IS FREE.
  §KEEP§ IT IS THE SMALLER OF THE TWO FLOORS THAT GOVERNS A
  CLAIM ABOUT A DIFFERENCE BETWEEN ARMS.
  §KEEP§ THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS REPORT PRINTED ONLY THE
  §KEEP§ CHASE AND THEREFORE CALLED A 1.12x NOT A DIFFERENCE,
  §KEEP§ WHICH WAS WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION.

  ESTIMATOR FLOOR (the pointer chase)    2.157 ticks/op
  COMPARISON FLOOR (worst pass-to-pass)  0.976 ticks/op
  THE COST OF A SPILLED VALUE, against the COMPARISON
  floor, WHICH IS THE SMALLER OF THE TWO:
    1 spilled   +0.557 ticks/op   1.20x  at or below the floor, read it as zero
    2 spilled   +0.531 ticks/op   1.19x  at or below the floor, read it as zero
    4 spilled   +1.115 ticks/op   1.40x  ABOVE the floor, so this IS a difference

  CHECKSUMS: 5925179420309322629 5925179420309322629 5925179420309322629 5925179420309322629
  all four AGREE, so all four did the work

========================================================================
SECTION 3 -- TWO SCHEDULES OF THE SAME INDEPENDENT
OPERATIONS, AND WHETHER INTERLEAVING THEM IS WORTH
ANYTHING ON THIS MACHINE.
========================================================================

  schedule               ticks/op   vs serial   checksum
  serial (one chain)        2.060       1.00x   -6711964969805938688
  interleaved (four)        2.060       1.00x   -6711964969805938688

  difference +0.000 ticks/op, against a floor of 2.157: AT OR BELOW the floor -- on this machine this schedule CHANGE IS NOT A RESULT

  CHECKSUMS AGREE, so the reorder is legal

